-- title:
--   Oriented étale crossing charts uv=p^e on the Deligne–Rapoport model
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and $M$ with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, and a subgroup $H \le (\mathbf Z/M)^\times$ containing every unit whose image in $(\mathbf Z/(M/p))^\times$ is $1$; let `hj` assert that the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` (the Laurent series $q^{-1}$ times the integral numerator series of $j$) lies in `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`, the subfield of $\mathbf Q((q))$ generated by the integral form ratios for $SL(2,\mathbf Z)$, and let $\mathfrak X$ be a term of `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`, i.e. a bundle of properties of the two-chart integral model `X p (ΓM M H) hj` over $\operatorname{Spec}(R\,p)$ (properness, flatness, integrality, local finite presentation and normality of `toBase`, properness and relative-dimension-one smoothness at level `ΓN`, a smooth proper curve model over $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ identified with the geometric generic fibre together with Galois- and $q$-expansion-compatibility, and further conditions). Let $A \subseteq \overline{\mathbf Q}$ be a valuation subring with $p$ a non-unit of $A$, with algebraically closed residue field of characteristic $p$, and $\rho : R\,p \to A$ a lift of the structure map $R\,p \to \overline{\mathbf Q}$. Let $O$ be a discrete valuation domain with $\mathfrak m_O = (p)$, equipped with $\rho_O : R\,p \to O$ and $\mathrm{to}\kappa : O \to \kappa_A$ satisfying $\mathrm{to}\kappa \circ \rho_O = \mathrm{res}_A \circ \rho$. Let $\mathrm{bc}$ be a morphism from the geometric special fibre `fibre ((residue A).comp ρ)` to the model $\mathfrak X_O :=$ `pullback (toBase p (ΓM M H) hj) (Spec.map ρO)`, compatible with both projections (over `X` on the nose, and over the base after composing with $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{to}\kappa)$). Write $C$ for the fibre product of the two morphisms `𝔛.comp A hA ρ hρ 0` and `𝔛.comp A hA ρ hρ 1` into the geometric special fibre, and for $n \in C$ let $x_n$ denote the image of $n$ under the first projection followed by `𝔛.comp … 0` followed by $\mathrm{bc}$. Assume every $x_n$ is $O/p$-rational, i.e. for each $x \in C$ there is a morphism $s : \operatorname{Spec}(O/\mathfrak m_O) \to \mathfrak X_O$ over $\operatorname{Spec} O$ (that is, $s$ followed by the projection to the base is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the residue map of $O$) sending the closed point to $x_x$. The conclusion: for every $n \in C$ there are an integer $e \ge 1$, an open subscheme $U \subseteq \mathfrak X_O$ containing $x_n$, and a morphism $f : U \to \operatorname{Spec}\bigl(O[X_0,X_1]/(X_0X_1 - p^e)\bigr)$ such that: $f$ is a morphism over $\operatorname{Spec} O$ ($f$ followed by $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $O \to O[X_0,X_1]/(X_0X_1-p^e)$ equals the inclusion of $U$ followed by the projection of $\mathfrak X_O$ to $\operatorname{Spec} O$); for $y \in U$, both distinguished elements `CrossingQuotient.U (p^e)` and `CrossingQuotient.V (p^e)` lie in the prime $f(y)$ exactly when the image of $y$ is $x_n$, so the fibre over the vertex is the single point $x_n$; at every $y \in U$ above $x_n$ the stalk map of $f$ is flat, sends the maximal ideal onto the maximal ideal and induces an isomorphism of residue fields, and some open $V \ni y$ in $U$ has $V \hookrightarrow U$ followed by $f$ étale; and $f$ is oriented, in that for $y \in U$ the condition `CrossingQuotient.V (p^e) ∈ f(y)` holds if and only if the image of $y$ lies in the range of the base map of `𝔛.comp … 0` followed by $\mathrm{bc}$, and likewise `CrossingQuotient.U (p^e) ∈ f(y)` if and only if that image lies in the range of `𝔛.comp … 1` followed by $\mathrm{bc}$ (each equivalence stated as two implications).
--
--   This is the local structure of the Deligne–Rapoport model of $X_H(M)$, $p \parallel M$, at a crossing point of its special fibre: an étale-local presentation $uv = p^e$ over the discrete valuation ring $O$, with the two branches matched to the two components meeting at the crossing. It is the form used when Hensel-lifting $A$-valued sections through a crossing and when computing widths of the annuli attached at both ends of a component.
